WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.961 --> 00:00:14.050
If you've ever moved out of an apartment

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:16.960
or house, chances are you've encountered

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:18.600
one of these boxes.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.231
It's not surprising since Home Depot

00:00:20.231 --> 00:00:22.300
has the largest market share

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:27.300
for moving boxes, selling 75
million of them each year.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:31.660
Home Depot boxes are made
exclusively by Pratt Industries

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:34.650
at five different paper mills in the US.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.460
What may surprise you is
that the whole process

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:39.096
begins at your front steps.

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:48.630
First, recycled paper gets
picked up from the city curbs

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:51.520
and brought to the
Staten Island paper mill

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:53.400
by barge or truck.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.320
John: One thing that is very
significant about our business

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:59.100
overall is that we only make things

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:01.060
out of recycled material,
we don't make anything

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:02.342
at all out of virgin trees.

00:01:02.342 --> 00:01:04.700
Narrator: Then there are six main steps

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:08.783
in turning recycled material
into a corrugated box.

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:17.560
So once the recyclable
paper has been unloaded,

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:19.340
a large crane then comes down and lifts it

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:20.770
and puts it up into the pulper.

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:21.930
The easiest way to explain this

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:24.570
is if you think about a
regular washing machine

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:26.270
where you have an agitator in the middle,

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:28.130
we put the recyclable paper in there,

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:30.970
and then it's agitated
along with warm water

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:32.320
to separate the fibers out.

00:01:36.265 --> 00:01:37.730
Narrator: Once the paper is broken down

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:39.670
into pulp, it goes through several

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:42.030
screening-and-filtering processes

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:44.247
to remove impurities such as

00:01:44.247 --> 00:01:48.080
ink, plastic, rubber, and styrofoam.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:51.613
What's left is a very
clean, raw paper fiber.

00:01:56.850 --> 00:02:00.050
The raw paper fiber is
then pressed and vacuumed

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:05.050
into paper and then turned
into a 35-ton paper roll.

00:02:12.558 --> 00:02:14.730
Three separate rolls of
paper are run through

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:16.680
a corrugating machine to create

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:18.083
a corrugated board.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:23.990
There are two outsides and a wavy center.

00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:26.580
The fluted middle is what gives the boxes

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:30.263
their crush resistence
and stacking strength.

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:34.860
The corrugated sheet is then cut

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.110
into three different widths depending on

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:38.820
the size of the box,

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:40.790
and each box is stamped with

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:43.073
Home Depot's iconic logo.

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:49.420
Lastly, the box is glued and shipped off

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:51.623
to be folded into a finished box.

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:54.570
John: Arguably one of
the most common things

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:56.580
that's just so in the background,

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:57.840
no one ever pays any attention to it,

00:02:57.840 --> 00:02:59.910
but there's almost nothing that doesn't

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:01.620
arrive at your store or your door

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:02.940
one way or the other in some

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:05.166
kind of corrugated box, and so

00:03:05.166 --> 00:03:07.201
you know, if you think about purely

00:03:07.201 --> 00:03:09.010
with the rise of e-commerce, it's just

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:10.879
becoming more and more common,

00:03:10.879 --> 00:03:13.220
and so it's great to be able to do

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:14.560
something productive with that

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:16.820
and turn it back into a usable product.

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:18.068
Our program through the Home Depot

00:03:18.068 --> 00:03:20.800
saves about 1.4 million trees a year

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:24.150
and then 570 million
gallons of fresh water.

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:27.290
The recycle process uses
one-third of the amount of water

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:28.863
that a regular process would use.

